1	# Rust API
2	
3	## Crate Root (`michi_core`)
4	
5	`michi-core` is the one crate holding every sync primitive. Each primitive is a module (`michi_core::toon`, `michi_core::kv`, `michi_core::truncate`, `michi_core::resilience`, …), and the most-used types are re-exported at the crate root:
6	
7	```rust
8	pub use error::{DomainError, ErrorClass, ErrorCode, Sensitive, MAY_HAVE_TAKEN_EFFECT_HINT};
9	pub use response::{AgentResponse, OutputFormat};
10	pub use kv::{render_kv, KvError, KvItem, KvValue};
11	pub use audience::Audience;
12	pub use hints::{append_hints, render_hints, Hint};
13	pub use idempotency::{CompletedOp, DuplicateOpIndex, FailedOp, PartialSuccess, SkippedOp};
14	pub use mcp::{CallToolResult, ContentBlock, JsonObject, StructuredContentError};
15	pub use recovery::{append_recovery, render_recovery, RecoveryHint};
16	pub use status::{Health, StatusItem, StatusResponse};
17	pub use resilience::{
18	    already_done, is_retryable_status, next_retry_delay, parse_retry_after, parse_retry_after_at,
19	    render_already_done, AlreadyDone, IdempotencyKey, RetryBudget, RetryConfig, RetryConfigError,
20	};
21	pub use truncate::{truncate, Truncated};
22	pub use truncation::{truncate_by_count, truncate_by_size, TruncationOutcome};
23	```
24	
25	There is no crate-wide `Error` enum or `Result` alias: every fallible function returns its own error type (`ToonError`, `KvError`, `RetryConfigError`, `StructuredContentError`, `DuplicateOpIndex`, …), and `DomainError` is the one agent-facing error.
26	
27	### API conventions
28	
29	One rule for every builder and getter, so 1.0 freezes one shape (see "Invariants live behind private fields" in [06-decisions.md](06-decisions.md)):
30	
31	- **Builders** consume `self`, are `#[must_use]` and are named for what they set, with no prefix: `x(v)` sets one value or appends one entry (`hint`, `cause`, `recovery`, `retryable`); the plural `xs(vec)` replaces a whole list (`hints`).
32	- **Getters** take `&self` and are named for the field; a `bool` reads `is_x()`. When a builder of the same type already uses the field's name, the getter is `get_x()`, as in `std::process::Command::get_args` (`DomainError::get_recovery`, `get_retry_after`).
33	- **Construction:** `new` never fails; a constructor that checks is `try_new` and returns its own error type. Arguments go identity first (`code`, `index`, `id`, `key`), then name or text, then data; anything optional is a builder call.
34	- **Hints** are passed as `&[Hint]` (a slice) or `Vec<Hint>` (a replacing builder), never `&[String]`.
35	- **Rendering:** a type with more than one output format renders through `render(OutputFormat)`; a text-only type has `render()`. Other targets get their own named method (`render_github_annotation`).
36	- **Fields:** a type whose fields carry a cross-field invariant keeps them private (`DomainError`, `RetryConfig`, `PartialSuccess`, `Section`); a plain record keeps them public (`KvItem`, `FailedOp`, `CompletedOp`, `SkippedOp`, `RecoveryHint`, `StatusItem`, `StatusResponse`, `PipelineStep`, `Pipeline`, `CallToolResult`).
37	
38	The root re-exports are explicit lists (0.4.0 replaced the `resilience::*` and `truncate::*` globs), so a new item in a module does not appear at the root by accident. `toon`, `pipeline` and `telemetry` are not re-exported at the root; their items are reached as `michi_core::toon::ToonOptions`, `michi_core::toon::list()`, `michi_core::pipeline::StepStatus`, and so on. There is no `michi` crate (the name on crates.io belongs to an unrelated project); consumers who prefer `michi::` paths rename the dependency with `michi = { package = "michi-core", version = "0.7" }`.
39	
40	## `toon` — List Rendering
41	
42	`michi_core::toon` formats tabular lists using Token-Oriented Object Notation (**AXI P1**). Small cell strings (≤ 24 bytes) are stack-inlined via `compact_str::CompactString`.
43	
44	`ToonOptions` and `render_toon` write one list: the entry and the `truncated` block for any cut cell. `totalCount`, `help[]` and the truncation hint are the composer's (`AgentResponse`), which places each once however many sections a response has, so the standalone renderer takes none of them. To add hints to a standalone list, use `append_hints`.
45	
46	```rust
47	#[non_exhaustive]
48	pub struct ToonOptions {
49	    pub type_name: String,
50	    pub fields: Vec<String>,
51	    pub rows: Vec<Vec<Value>>,
52	    /// Max string cell length in chars, counted before escaping/quoting (default 200). A longer cell keeps its
53	    /// first `max_cell_len - 1` chars and ends in a plain `…`; each cut is listed in a
54	    /// `truncated[N]{row,field,removed}:` block after the list.
55	    pub max_cell_len: usize,
56	}
57	
58	impl ToonOptions {
59	    pub fn new(type_name: impl Into<String>, fields: Vec<String>, rows: Vec<Vec<Value>>) -> Self;
60	    pub fn max_cell_len(self, len: usize) -> Self;
61	    /// Validate structural invariants before rendering; rendering consumes the returned proof.
62	    /// Returns Err if type_name is a reserved document key (`totalCount`, `help`, `recovery`, `truncated`,
63	    /// `budget`, `sections`, `provenance`, `error`, `message`, `causes`, `exit_code`),
64	    /// there are more than MAX_FIELDS fields, a field name is repeated, rows × fields is more than
65	    /// MAX_CELLS, or any row's length differs from fields.len() (checked in that order; `AgentResponse` also
66	    /// checks its `truncation_hint` against MAX_TRUNCATION_HINT_CHARS chars, before the row lengths). Any other name is valid and is quoted per TOON 4.4 §7.3.
67	    pub fn validate(&self) -> Result<ToonDocument<'_>, ToonError>;
68	}
