193	    pub fn label(&self) -> &'static str;           // snake_case: "invalid_input", "not_found", …
194	    pub fn is_retryable_by_default(&self) -> bool; // RateLimited, Unavailable
195	    pub fn may_have_taken_effect(&self) -> bool;   // Timeout
196	    pub fn default_class(&self) -> ErrorClass;     // User / Internal / Ambiguous (Timeout)
197	}
198	
199	#[non_exhaustive]
200	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
201	pub enum ErrorClass { User, Internal, Transient, Ambiguous }
202	
203	pub const MAY_HAVE_TAKEN_EFFECT_HINT: &str =
204	    "the operation may have taken effect; verify whether it completed before retrying, unless it is idempotent";
205	
206	/// Omits its content from Debug and Display (both print "<redacted>").
207	pub struct Sensitive<T>(pub T);
208	```
209	
210	- `render(OutputFormat::Text)` outputs a TOON 4.4 document: `error: <code>`, `message: …` (quoted per §7.2), `causes[N]: …` when there are causes, `exit_code: 1`, then `help[N]: …` and `recovery[1]{tool,params,reason}:` when set. See [02-toon-format.md](02-toon-format.md#agent-facing-blocks).
211	- `render_github_annotation()` outputs a GitHub Actions annotation (`::error title=code::message`). The message is encoded as workflow command data (`%` → `%25` first, then CR → `%0D`, LF → `%0A`); the title also encodes `:` and `,`.
212	- `retryable` means calling again is expected to help without repeating an effect. `DomainError::new` sets it from `ErrorCode::is_retryable_by_default()`: `true` for `RateLimited` and `Unavailable` (the request was not processed), `false` for every other code. A `Timeout` or `ExternalFailure` may have taken effect, so mark it `.retryable(true)` only when the operation is idempotent. `is_retryable()` and `class() == ErrorClass::Transient` always agree.
213	- A `Timeout` that is not marked retryable is `ErrorClass::Ambiguous` (no answer came back, so the operation may or may not have taken effect: not the caller's fault and not an internal error), and `DomainError::new` gives it the hint `MAY_HAVE_TAKEN_EFFECT_HINT` ("the operation may have taken effect; verify whether it completed before retrying, unless it is idempotent"). `.retryable(true)` makes it `Transient` and removes that hint; `.retryable(false)` puts it back. `ExternalFailure` covers too much (a failed local walk, a server error) to say, so it stays `Internal` with no automatic hint; add your own when you know the operation may have applied.
214	- `render(OutputFormat::Json)` outputs a JSON object: `{"isError":true,"error":"...","message":"...","causes":[...],"retryable":bool,"hints":[...],"recovery":null|{...}}`. `causes` and `hints` are always present, `[]` when empty; serde and the `schemars` schema match it.
215	- `from_error(code, err)` sets `message` to `err.to_string()` and nothing else: the `source()` chain stays out of the agent's view because causes often carry URLs with tokens, file paths and other internals. The top-level `Display` is still agent-visible, so check it too, or use `DomainError::new` with a message you wrote. It is passed through `redact_common_shapes` first (best effort, see `redact`).
216	- `from_error_with_causes(code, err)` is the opt-in: it does what `from_error` does, then walks `err.source()`, adding each link's `Display` to `causes` (the separate diagnosis field, never `help[]`). A link whose text the last kept text (the message or the previous kept cause) already contains is skipped, since `thiserror` messages often embed their source (`#[error("walk failed: {0}")]`). The walk stops after 128 links. Nothing is stored but text: `source()` on a `DomainError` stays `None`, and it stays `Clone + Send + Sync`. Line breaks stay inside their cause; `render()` escapes them.
217	- With the `miette` feature, `Diagnostic::help()` lists the hints, then one `caused by: …` line per cause (`None` when there are neither). The GitHub annotation stays message-only.
218	- `to_call_tool_result()` builds an MCP `CallToolResult` with `is_error:true` and the rendered JSON as `structured_content`.
219	- When the `schemars` feature is enabled, `DomainError` implements schemars 1.x's `JsonSchema` (the schema of the JSON render's object, as JSON Schema 2020-12). Only types serde actually serializes have a schema: `StatusResponse`, `ToonOptions`, `ErrorClass` and the other render-only types do not.
220	
221	### `AgentResponse` Builder
222	
223	`AgentResponse` composes an ordered list of sections (TOON lists, key-value blocks, text), hints, recovery hints and audience routing. It is a **consuming** builder — every setter takes `self` by value and returns `Self`, so calls chain (`AgentResponse::new("t").items(...).hint(...)`), not `&mut self` mutation:
224	
225	```rust
226	/// The default TOON cell cap, in chars (`michi_core::response::DEFAULT_TRUNCATE_CELLS`; not at the root).
227	pub const DEFAULT_TRUNCATE_CELLS: usize = 200;
228	
229	#[non_exhaustive]
230	#[derive(Default)]
231	pub enum OutputFormat { #[default] Text, Json }
232	
233	#[non_exhaustive]
234	pub struct AgentResponse { ... }
235	
236	impl AgentResponse {
237	    pub fn new(type_name: impl Into<String>) -> Self;
238	    /// Sugar for `section(Section::toon(type_name, fields, rows))`. Every call appends.
239	    pub fn items(self, rows: Vec<Vec<Value>>, fields: &[&str]) -> Self;
240	    /// Sugar for `section(Section::kv(type_name, items))`. Every call appends.
241	    pub fn kv_items(self, items: Vec<KvItem>) -> Self;
242	    /// Appends a section; sections render in call order.
243	    pub fn section(self, section: Section) -> Self;
244	    /// Appends a `Toon` section whose rows are `outcome.kept` (each mapped by `row`) and whose total is
245	    /// `outcome.total_before`, and `outcome.hint(guidance)` to the hints only when something was cut.
246	    pub fn truncated_section<T>(
247	        self,
248	        name: impl Into<String>,
249	        fields: Vec<String>,
250	        outcome: &TruncationOutcome<T>,
251	        row: impl FnMut(&T) -> Vec<Value>,
252	        guidance: impl Into<String>,
253	    ) -> Self;
254	    pub fn hint(self, hint: impl Into<String>) -> Self;
255	    /// Replaces the full hint list (contrast `hint()`, which appends).
256	    pub fn hints(self, hints: Vec<Hint>) -> Self;
257	    pub fn recovery_hint(self, r: RecoveryHint) -> Self;
258	    /// Overrides the default TOON cell truncation limit (200 chars, counted before escaping/quoting); every `Toon`
259	    /// section, never `Kv` or `Text`.
260	    pub fn truncate_cells_at(self, limit: usize) -> Self;
261	    /// The caller's "get it in full" instruction for every `Toon` section; unset by default. When a cell was cut,
262	    /// michi adds one `help[]` line naming the `truncated` block and this instruction (after your own hints, once for
263	    /// the response). At most MAX_TRUNCATION_HINT_CHARS chars. Tool-author text, never data.
264	    pub fn truncation_hint(self, hint: impl Into<String>) -> Self;
265	    pub fn as_error(self) -> Self;
266	    pub fn human_content(self, text: impl Into<String>) -> Self;
267	
268	    /// The one render path: every section in call order, then `budget:`, `provenance:`, `help[]`, `recovery[]`.
269	    pub fn render(&self, format: OutputFormat) -> String;
270	    /// Just the `help[N]: …` document for `.hint()`/`.hints()`.
271	    pub fn render_hints_only(&self) -> String;
272	    /// `as_error()` was called, or a section's TOON rows / KV items fail validation. Validates without rendering.
273	    pub fn is_error(&self) -> bool;
274	    /// `"user"` falls back to `render(OutputFormat::Text)` when `.human_content()` was never set.
275	    pub fn render_for(&self, audience: Audience) -> String;
276	    pub fn has_human_content(&self) -> bool;
277	    pub fn to_call_tool_result(&self) -> CallToolResult;
278	}
279	```
280	
281	`render_json()` is not part of the public API — it's reached only via `render(OutputFormat::Json)`.
282	
283	A response keeps its body in one place, an ordered `Vec<Section>`. `items()` and `kv_items()` are shorthand that append a section named for the response's type, so mixing them renders every body in call order. Because a section is a root key of the document, the shorthand serves one list (and one KV block) per response: a second `items()` call repeats the key and renders the `toon_validation_failed` error, never invalid TOON. For several lists use `section(Section::toon(distinct_name, ..))`. There is no response-level `total_count`: a total belongs to the section it counts, set with `Section::total_count`. `is_error()` validates without rendering. Hints and recovery are appended to the rendered body in place. `render(OutputFormat::Json)` is `{"body":…,"sections":[…],"hints":[…],"recovery":[…],"isError":bool}`, the same object `to_call_tool_result()` puts in `structured_content`; `sections` appears once the response has a section.
284	
285	A response with no section, budget or provenance renders `type_name: []` and `totalCount: 0`, the definitive empty list: the same text an empty `Toon` section renders, and the only empty-state API (there is no `empty_state()`). An empty KV section renders nothing (or `totalCount: n` when a larger total is set).
286	
287	`truncated_section(name, fields, &outcome, row, guidance)` builds the section from `outcome.kept` (each item turned into a row by `row`), sets its total to `outcome.total_before` and adds the cut hint, in one call. Because michi builds the rows itself, the list's `[N]`, its `totalCount`, the JSON `returnedCount`/`truncated` and the `showing N of M` hint cannot describe different cuts.
288	
289	### Budget and provenance types (`budget`, `provenance`)
290	
291	Plain data types for the envelope in [07-wisp-and-monokl-envelope.md](07-wisp-and-monokl-envelope.md) §2 and §3. All three are `#[non_exhaustive]`, derive `Debug`, `Clone`, `Default` (where it makes sense), `PartialEq` and `Eq`, and have builder methods. Behind `serde` they derive `Serialize` with camelCase keys (absent `Option`s skipped); behind `schemars` they derive `JsonSchema`.
292	
293	- `BudgetSummary`: optional `max_tokens`, `max_bytes`, `max_items`, `tokens_used`, `bytes_used`; `truncated: bool` (the budget dropped something the sections cannot show). Builders: `new`, `max_tokens`, `max_bytes`, `max_items`, `tokens_used`, `bytes_used`, `truncated`. It holds only what michi cannot know: there is no per-section list (see below).
294	- `ProvenanceBlock`: ordered `entries: Vec<ProvenanceEntry>` and optional `fingerprint`. Builders: `new`, `entry(label, value)`, `fingerprint`.
295	- `ProvenanceEntry`: `label`, `value`; `new(label, value)`.
296	
297	Attach them with `AgentResponse::budget(BudgetSummary)` and `AgentResponse::provenance(ProvenanceBlock)` (see [Envelope on `AgentResponse`](#envelope-on-agentresponse)). The `budget:` block lists `tokensUsed`, `bytesUsed`, `maxTokens`, `maxBytes`, `maxItems` when set, then `truncated`, which is true when the caller set it or any section of the response was cut. The `sections[n]{name,returned,total,truncated}:` roll-up follows only when the response has two or more sections, and michi writes it from those sections at render time: one row per section, its name, `count()`, total (an unknown `total` is a `null` cell) and `truncated()`. It cannot name a section the response does not hold. The budget's JSON is the numbers and `truncated`; the response's `sections` array already carries each section's counts. The `provenance:` block lists entries in order, then `fingerprint`. Labels are encoded as TOON keys and values as TOON strings, so newlines and colons in caller text cannot start a line of their own. A label may not be `fingerprint`, with or without a fingerprint set.
298	
299	### Sections (`section`)
300	
301	A response holds its body as sections. `AgentResponse::section(Section)` appends one; sections render in call order, before `budget:`, `provenance:`, `help[]` and `recovery[]`. The caller owns the order. `items()` and `kv_items()` are shorthand for a `Toon` or `Kv` section named for the response's type, so there is one storage model and one render path.
302	
303	```rust
304	#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
305	pub struct Section { /* private: name, body, total */ }
306	
307	#[non_exhaustive] #[derive(Debug, Clone)]
308	pub enum SectionBody {
309	    Toon { fields: Vec<String>, rows: Vec<Vec<Value>> },
310	    Kv(Vec<KvItem>),
311	    Text { text: String, count: usize },
312	}
313	
314	impl Section {
315	    pub fn toon(name: impl Into<String>, fields: Vec<String>, rows: Vec<Vec<Value>>) -> Self;
316	    pub fn kv(name: impl Into<String>, items: Vec<KvItem>) -> Self;
317	    /// Count 1 for non-empty text, 0 for empty.
318	    pub fn text(name: impl Into<String>, body: impl Into<String>) -> Self;
319	    /// A text section that stands for `count` items.
320	    pub fn text_with_count(name: impl Into<String>, body: impl Into<String>, count: usize) -> Self;
321	    /// The one fact a caller sets: how many existed before a cut.
322	    pub fn total_count(self, total: Option<usize>) -> Self;
323	    pub fn name(&self) -> &str;
324	    pub fn body(&self) -> &SectionBody;
325	    /// rows.len() / items.len() for Toon and Kv, the constructor's count for Text.
326	    pub fn count(&self) -> usize;
327	    pub fn total(&self) -> Option<usize>;
328	    /// Derived: total() > count().
329	    pub fn truncated(&self) -> bool;
330	}
331	```
332	
333	- `Toon` bodies render as a TOON list named `name`, with the response's `truncate_cells_at` cap and `truncation_hint` applied. `Kv` bodies render as `key: value` lines (the name is not printed). `Text` bodies are one string value, `name: <escaped value>`, escaped like any TOON string and never raw, so prose cannot forge a `help[]` line. A `totalCount` renders for a `Text` section as for the others.
334	- `totalCount: n` follows a section only when `total_count` is `Some(n)` with `n` above the section's count: a total equal to the rows returned says nothing new.
335	- The fields are private, and each fact has one source: the count is the body's rows or items (a `Text` body carries the count its constructor was given), the total is what `total_count` set, and `truncated()` is `total > count`. Text and JSON read the same three, so a section cannot print `totalCount: 5` while its JSON says `"truncated": false`.
336	- An empty `Toon` section is the definitive empty state: `name: []` and `totalCount: 0` (or the larger `totalCount: n` the caller set); it takes part in the merged `totalCount` table like any section with a total. The JSON `sections` entry keeps the caller's facts (`returnedCount: 0`, and a `totalCount` only when one was set). An empty `Kv` section renders nothing (or `totalCount: n` when a larger total is set).
337	- A section that fails validation renders an `error:` block (`toon_validation_failed` or `kv_validation_failed`) and makes `is_error()` true.
338	- `render(Json)` adds a `sections` array; see [Envelope on `AgentResponse`](#envelope-on-agentresponse). Cut-cell `help[]` lines from every section merge into the one `help` block, once.
339	
340	### Envelope on `AgentResponse`
341	
342	```rust
343	impl AgentResponse {
344	    /// One per response; a second call replaces the first.
345	    #[must_use] pub fn budget(self, b: BudgetSummary) -> Self;
346	    /// One per response; a second call replaces the first.
347	    #[must_use] pub fn provenance(self, p: ProvenanceBlock) -> Self;
348	}
349	```
350	
351	Text order: the sections in call order, `budget:`, `provenance:`, `help[]`, `recovery[]`. A response with no section, budget or provenance renders `type: []`. With a budget or provenance and no section it renders no empty list, so `AgentResponse::new("t").budget(b)` is just the `budget:` block.
352	
353	- The budget roll-up (`sections[n]{...}`) renders only when the response has two or more sections, and is derived from them.
354	- `truncated` and `totalCount` are root keys, and a TOON object may not repeat a key. When one block has cut cells (or a `totalCount`) it renders inline after its list. When two or more do, each becomes one table after the last block, with a leading `section` column: `truncated[N]{section,row,field,removed}:` and `totalCount[N]{section,total}:`. michi's own "Cut cells are listed in the truncated block" help line is added once.
355	- Section names and KV keys are root keys, beside the names michi writes itself. Those are one reserved list shared by every writer: `totalCount`, `help`, `recovery`, `truncated`, `budget`, `sections`, `provenance`, and the error document's `error`, `message`, `causes` and `exit_code`, reserved even on success so caller data cannot render an error document while `isError` is false. Validity never depends on caller data: a reserved name, two blocks writing one root key, two failed sections (each would write `error`), or two equal provenance labels (or a label `fingerprint`, with or without a fingerprint) renders the whole body as the `toon_validation_failed` (or `kv_validation_failed`) error naming the key, and `is_error()`, the JSON `isError` and MCP `isError` are true. The JSON `sections` still carry the data as given.
356	- JSON (`render(Json)` and `structured_content`): `body`, then `sections`, `budget`, `provenance` only when set (`sections` is set by `items()` and `kv_items()` too), then `hints`, `recovery`, `isError`. A section is `{name, kind, ...}` with `kind` `toon` (`fields`, `rows` of typed values), `kv` (`items` of `{key, value}`) or `text` (`text`), then `returnedCount`, `totalCount` when set, and `truncated`. Rows carry the full values: the cell cap (`truncate_cells_at`) shapes only the text render, and `structured_content` is the canonical, uncapped data. Keys are camelCase and hand-built, so the default build needs no `serde_json`.
